.Dd July 5, 2026
.Dt SD 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sd
.Nd Secure-Enclave-backed SSH key, agent, and loader
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm ssh-keygen
.Op Fl f Ar keyfile
.Op Fl C Ar comment
.Op Fl t Cm ecdsa
.Op Fl y
.Nm
.Cm ssh-agent
.Op Fl a Ar socket
.Op Fl s | Fl c
.Op Fl d | Fl k
.Op Ar provider ...
.Nm
.Cm ssh-add
.Op Fl a Ar socket
.Oo Fl d | Fl D | Fl l | Fl L Oc
.Op Ar keyfile ...
.Nm
.Cm ssh-copy-id
.Op Fl i Ar keyfile
.Op Ar option ...
.Ar [user@]host
.Nm
.Cm install
.Op Fl a Ar socket
.Nm
.Cm uninstall
.Nm
.Cm doctor
.Nm
.Cm setup-git-signing
.Op Fl -no-auto-sign-commits
.Op Fl -no-auto-sign-tags
.Op Fl -email Ar email
.Op Fl -github-user Ar user
.Op Fl -key Ar keyfile
.Op Fl -allowed-signers Ar file
.Op Fl y
.Op Fl -force
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
keeps an SSH authentication key in the Secure Enclave and serves it to stock
OpenSSH over the ssh-agent protocol.
The private key is generated inside the Secure Enclave and never leaves it;
Touch ID is required for every signature.
The commands mirror the OpenSSH tools they imitate.
.Ss sd ssh-keygen
Create a Secure-Enclave P-256 key: an opaque handle file plus a standard
.Pa .pub
public-key line.
.Bl -tag -width "-C comment"
.It Fl f Ar keyfile
Output keyfile (default
.Pa ~/.ssh/id_sod ) .
.It Fl C Ar comment
Comment for the public-key line (default
.Ar user@host ) .
.It Fl t Cm ecdsa
Key type; only
.Cm ecdsa
is accepted (the Secure Enclave is P-256 only).
.It Fl y
Read a handle file and print its public-key line.
.El
.Ss sd ssh-agent
Run the agent on a unix socket.
With no options it ensures an agent is running (reusing one if present) and
prints shell commands to set
.Ev SSH_AUTH_SOCK :
.Dl eval \&"$(sd ssh-agent)\&"
.Pp
The default key
.Pa ~/.ssh/id_sod
is served automatically; no
.Cm sd ssh-add
is needed.
Drop it for the session with
.Fl d
or
.Fl D .
.Bl -tag -width "-a socket"
.It Fl a Ar socket
Socket path (default
.Pa ~/.ssh/sod-agent.sock ) .
.It Fl s | Fl c
Force Bourne-shell (sh) or C-shell (csh) env output (default: detect from
.Ev SHELL ) .
.It Fl d
Foreground mode: bind and serve, do not fork (debugging / LaunchAgent).
.It Fl k
Kill the agent running on the socket.
.It Fl -allow-agent-forwarding
Permit agent forwarding
.Pq Cm ssh Fl A .
Off by default: the agent refuses to sign for, or list keys over, a forwarded
connection, so a remote host cannot use this Secure-Enclave key.
.Cm ProxyJump
.Pq Fl J
and direct connections are unaffected either way.
.El
.Pp
To run the agent automatically at login, see
.Cm sd install
below.
.Ss sd ssh-add
Load, unload, or list keys.
With no arguments, loads the default key
.Pa ~/.ssh/id_sod .
.Bl -tag -width "-a socket"
.It Fl a Ar socket
Agent socket (default
.Ev SSH_AUTH_SOCK ,
else
.Pa ~/.ssh/sod-agent.sock ) .
.It Fl d , Fl e
Unload the given key(s).
.It Fl D
Unload all keys.
.It Fl l , Fl L
List loaded keys as fingerprints
.Pq Fl l
or full public keys
.Pq Fl L .
.El
.Ss sd ssh-copy-id
Authorize your key on a server by appending its public key to the remote
.Pa authorized_keys .
A thin wrapper over
.Xr ssh-copy-id 1
that defaults the identity
.Pq Fl i
to
.Pa ~/.ssh/id_sod.pub
and forwards all other arguments unchanged, so
.Dl sd ssh-copy-id user@host
installs the Secure-Enclave public key on
.Ar host .
Pass your own
.Fl i
to override the default.
.Ss sd install
Set up everything in one step: if the default key
.Pa ~/.ssh/id_sod
does not exist, offer to create it, then install a per-user LaunchAgent
that runs
.Cm sd ssh-agent
on a fixed socket at login (restarting it if it exits).
The agent serves
.Pa ~/.ssh/id_sod
automatically, so no
.Cm sd ssh-add
is required.
Finally it prints the
.Ic echo
and
.Ic exec
commands to point your shell at the agent.
This is the recommended setup after installing
.Nm ;
it never edits your shell files itself.
.Bl -tag -width "-a socket"
.It Fl a Ar socket
Socket path (default
.Pa ~/.ssh/sod-agent.sock ) .
.El
.Pp
To use
.Nm
for only some hosts and keep your existing agent for the rest, skip the
.Ev SSH_AUTH_SOCK
line and instead set
.Cm IdentityAgent ~/.ssh/sod-agent.sock
per-host in
.Xr ssh_config 5 .
.Ss sd uninstall
Remove the LaunchAgent installed by
.Cm sd install .
.Ss sd doctor
Check the health of your setup and print actionable hints; read-only, it
inspects but never changes anything.
It reports whether the Secure Enclave is available, the default key
.Pa ~/.ssh/id_sod
exists, the login agent is installed and loaded, the agent socket is live and
holds your key, and whether
.Ev SSH_AUTH_SOCK
is set in the current shell and exported from your shell startup file.
It also checks whether
.Xr git 1
and
.Xr gh 1
are installed and, inside a repository, whether that repo's SSH-signing
config is coherent.
Exits non-zero if any check fails.
.Ss sd setup-git-signing
Configure the current repository to sign git tags and commits with your sod key
over SSH.
It sets
.Cm gpg.format=ssh ,
.Cm user.signingkey
to your
.Pa .pub ,
and
.Cm gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile ,
and appends your key to the allowed_signers file.
It configures the current repository only (no global config), prints a plan, and
asks before changing anything; it is idempotent and refuses to overwrite an
existing signing configuration without
.Fl -force .
By default it turns on both
.Cm commit.gpgsign
and
.Cm tag.gpgsign ,
so every commit and every annotated tag is signed automatically \(en each one a
Touch ID.
Opt out of either with
.Fl -no-auto-sign-commits
or
.Fl -no-auto-sign-tags ;
opting out of a bit this repo already has on turns it back off.
It never sets
.Cm user.name .
For GitHub's Verified badge the tag's email must be a verified GitHub email
(the tagger line is what GitHub checks).
If you already have a
.Cm user.email
configured (locally or globally) it is left untouched; otherwise it asks for
your GitHub username and sets a repo-local
.Cm user.email
of
.Ar user Ns @users.noreply.github.com ,
a verified no-reply address \(en so
.Fl -github-user Ar alice
maps to
.Cm user.email=alice@users.noreply.github.com .
Under
.Fl y
it cannot prompt, so pass
.Fl -github-user
or
.Fl -email .
.Pq If you do not know your GitHub username: Ic gh api user --jq .login .
.Bl -tag -width "-github-user user"
.It Fl -no-auto-sign-commits
Do not set
.Cm commit.gpgsign
(commits are auto-signed by default);
if this repo already has it on, turn it off.
.It Fl -no-auto-sign-tags
Do not set
.Cm tag.gpgsign
(annotated tags are auto-signed by default);
if this repo already has it on, turn it off.
.It Fl -email Ar email
Signer email: the
.Pa allowed_signers
principal, and \(en if you have no
.Cm user.email \(en
the value written to it.
Overrides the derived no-reply address.
.It Fl -github-user Ar user
GitHub username: sets
.Cm user.email
to
.Ar user Ns @users.noreply.github.com .
Consulted only when you have no
.Cm user.email
configured; required in that case under
.Fl y .
.It Fl -key Ar keyfile
Public key to sign with (default
.Pa ~/.ssh/id_sod.pub ) .
.It Fl -allowed-signers Ar file
The allowed_signers file to record your key in and point
.Cm gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile
at (default
.Pa ~/.ssh/allowed_signers ) .
.It Fl y
Assume yes: apply without prompting.
.It Fl -force
Overwrite a conflicting existing signing configuration.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width SSH_AUTH_SOCK
.It Ev SSH_AUTH_SOCK
Default agent socket for
.Cm ssh-add .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "~/.ssh/sod-agent.sock"
.It Pa ~/.ssh/id_sod
Default key handle.
.It Pa ~/.ssh/id_sod.pub
Default public key.
.It Pa ~/.ssh/sod-agent.sock
Default agent socket.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Set it up to run at login (recommended), then follow the printed instructions
and load the key:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sd ssh-keygen
sd install
sd ssh-add
.Ed
.Pp
Or run a throwaway agent in the current shell, load a key, and connect:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sd ssh-keygen -f ~/.ssh/id_sod
eval \&"$(sd ssh-agent)\&"
sd ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_sod
ssh -i ~/.ssh/id_sod user@host
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr gh 1 ,
.Xr git 1 ,
.Xr git-config 1 ,
.Xr ssh 1 ,
.Xr ssh-add 1 ,
.Xr ssh-agent 1 ,
.Xr ssh-copy-id 1 ,
.Xr ssh-keygen 1
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Botanica Software Labs
.Sh CAVEATS
macOS only.
The Secure Enclave requires Apple Silicon or a T2 chip.
.Pp
The agent does not enforce
.Xr ssh-add 1
key constraints \(em destination
.Pq Fl h ,
lifetime
.Pq Fl t ,
or confirm
.Pq Fl c .
A constrained add is
.Em refused
rather than honored as an unconstrained one, so a constraint that would not be
enforced never appears to succeed.
Touch ID gates every signature regardless.
.Pp
Agent forwarding
.Pq Cm ssh Fl A
is refused by default: a forwarded connection is presented an empty agent and
gets no signatures.
Use
.Fl -allow-agent-forwarding
to permit it.
Detection relies on the
.Ql session-bind@openssh.com
binding sent by OpenSSH 8.9 and later; older clients that forward an agent
cannot be distinguished.
.Pp
The Touch ID prompt names what is being signed and, when known, for what: the
repository containing the requesting process's working directory for git
signatures, or, for logins, the destination host from the requesting
.Xr ssh 1
command line together with the address of the TCP connection that
.Xr ssh 1
holds while it authenticates
.Pq its only one at that point; a ProxyJump destination has none .
This context is informational only \(em it is reported by the requesting
process, which any process running as the same user could fake \(em and is
meant to catch a mistimed tap, not to serve as a security control.
.Pp
.Cm sd setup-git-signing
never touches
.Cm user.name ,
only sets
.Cm user.email
when none is configured anywhere (it never overwrites an existing one), and
never overwrites an existing signing configuration without
.Fl -force .
By default it auto-signs both commits and annotated tags; opt out with
.Fl -no-auto-sign-commits
or
.Fl -no-auto-sign-tags .
